---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';
import { websiteJsonLd } from '../lib/seo';

const jsonLd = websiteJsonLd();
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="What Signal Daily is, how to read it, and what it deliberately does not do."
  path="/about"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <header class="sr-page-head">
    <p class="sr-micro sr-micro--accent">About</p>
    <h1>Daily news and authored writing across six categories.</h1>
    <p class="sr-page-head__lede">
      Signal Daily brings together sourced news in AI, BIO, GEO and MARKETS, with philosophy and
      classics in WISDOM and society, culture, education and work in SOCIETY.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="sr-prose">
    <h2>How to read the digest</h2>
    <p>
      Each day publishes up to four vertical editions (AI, bio, geo, markets). Every news story carries a
      three-line <strong>TL;DR</strong>, a short <strong>Why it matters</strong> framing, an{' '}
      <strong>editor’s note</strong> on what is still uncertain, and a list of{' '}
      <strong>sources</strong>. Numbers in square brackets in the body link straight to the
      underlying source.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>BRIEF</strong> — a fast read on one development.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>DEEP</strong> — analysis with more reporting and context.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>DATA</strong> — a numbers-led item, usually with a chart-friendly figure.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Cluster coverage</strong> — other outlets reporting the same story, so you can compare
        framing rather than trust a single summary.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Confidence</strong> — <em>high</em> means multiple independent primary sources agree;{' '}
        <em>medium</em> means the claim is credible but thin; <em>low</em> means it is developing and
        should be treated as provisional.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Features, analysis and essays</h2>
    <p>
      Manual writing can appear in any of the six categories. News-based features retain the
      digest’s citations and evidence labels. Standalone <strong>FEATURE</strong>,
      <strong>ANALYSIS</strong> and <strong>ESSAY</strong> pieces use a free-form body, a named
      author and a publication date. Their references support factual claims and direct quotations;
      personal experience and interpretation belong to the author. News confidence, related-report
      counts and three-line summaries are not required for these pieces.
    </p>
    <p>Explore published tags in each category. A language link opens the actual translation when one exists, or the other language’s home when it does not.</p>

    <h2>What it is not</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Not investment, medical or legal advice. Nothing here is a recommendation.</li>
      <li>Not a login product. There are no accounts, no paywall and no email capture.</li>
      <li>Not a tracking surface. No analytics scripts, no third-party embeds, no ad tech.</li>
      <li>Not a replacement for primary reporting. News and factual claims link to the documents or people behind them.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How it is produced</h2>
    <p>
      A collection pipeline gathers candidate items from a fixed registry of public sources. An AI
      model drafts a structured summary — headlines, TL;DRs, body blocks, tags and citation links. The
      publishing workflow validates the news structure and sources. Morning and evening automated
      editions cover the original four categories; manually commissioned features and essays publish
      when ready, including WISDOM and SOCIETY. Human editorial intervention and automated
      processing are distinct parts of this workflow.
      The full rules live in <a href="/standards">editorial standards</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>How it is built</h2>
    <p>
      A static Astro site. News ships as JSON and standalone writing as Markdown, pages are generated at build time, and the only
      client-side JavaScript covers the command palette, keyboard navigation, the light/dark theme and tag filtering. The full text of every page is in the HTML.
    </p>

    <h2>Archive and syndication</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="/archive">Archive</a> — standalone writing plus news editions by month and date.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/rss.xml">RSS</a> — all stories, newest first.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/llms.txt">/llms.txt</a> — machine-readable summary for language models.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/sources">Sources</a> — the registry the pipeline collects from.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Signal Daily is published at <a href={SITE.url}>{SITE.url.replace(/^https?:\/\//, '')}</a>. For
      corrections, see <a href="/standards">standards</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
